1. Log parser
Write a script that reads a log file (like /var/log/syslog format) and prints the count of lines per log level (ERROR, WARNING, INFO). Handle the case where the file doesn't exist gracefully.
2. Disk usage alert
Write a script that checks disk usage on / and prints a warning if usage is above 80%. Use shutil.disk_usage or subprocess calling df.
3. Process finder
Write a script that finds all running processes matching a given name (e.g. "nginx") and prints their PID and memory usage. You can shell out to ps or use the psutil library — try both ways if you have time.
4. Tail -f clone
Implement a simplified version of tail -f in Python: continuously monitor a file and print new lines as they're appended.
5. Bulk file organizer
Write a script that scans a directory and moves files into subfolders based on their extension (e.g. all .log files into logs/, .txt into text/).
6. Retry wrapper for a flaky command
Write a function that runs a shell command via subprocess, retries up to N times on failure with exponential backoff, and raises an exception if it never succeeds.
7. Find large files
Write a script that recursively scans a directory tree and prints the top 10 largest files, with human-readable sizes (KB/MB/GB).
8. Simple health-check daemon
Write a script that runs in a loop, pings a list of hosts (or checks if a port is open), and logs results with timestamps to a file. Bonus: make it exit cleanly on Ctrl+C (signal handling).
A few tips before you dive in:

Use subprocess.run(..., capture_output=True, text=True, check=True). <br>
Explaining why you chose subprocess vs a native Python library (e.g. shutil.disk_usage vs shelling out to df) — interviewers like hearing that tradeoff reasoning.<br>
Don't forget exception handling (FileNotFoundError, subprocess.CalledProcessError, PermissionError)